1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Insurer Solvency
- What it means and why it matters

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:13,000
What is solvency?
- Ability to meet obligations as they fall due
- Enough capital to absorb unexpected losses

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Why it matters
- Protects policyholders and beneficiaries
- Supports confidence in long-term promises
- Reduces systemic risk and contagion

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Regulatory frameworks
- {'EU': 'Solvency II (SCR & MCR)'}
- {'US': 'Risk-Based Capital (RBC)'}
- {'Principles': 'risk sensitivity, transparency, governance'}

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Capital requirements
- Target capital above minimum triggers
- SCR/MCR or RBC ratio thresholds
- Quality of capital (tiers / own funds)

6
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Major risk types
- Underwriting risk (pricing, reserves, catastrophe)
- Market and credit risk (rates, equity, spread, default)
- Operational and liquidity risk

7
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:58,000
ALM and reserves
- Match assets and liabilities by duration and cash flow
- Best-estimate liabilities plus prudence where required
- Sensitivity to interest rates and inflation

8
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:08,000
ORSA and governance
- Own Risk and Solvency Assessment (forward-looking)
- Stress and scenario testing across the cycle
- Risk appetite, limits, controls, culture

9
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Risk transfer and capital tools
- Reinsurance and retrocession
- Insurance-linked securities (cat bonds, sidecars)
- Capital raising, dividends, and buffers

10
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:26,000
What to watch
- SCR/RBC ratio trends and headroom to triggers
- Reserve adequacy and development
- Asset quality, liquidity, and ALM
- Reinsurance dependence and counterparty strength

11
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Wrap-up
- Strong capital + disciplined risk management = solvency
- {'Goal': 'protect policyholders through all conditions'}

